{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","requirement":"Run a functional backup every night from node2:/srv/inventory/data to dated archives under /var/backups/inventory on node1.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A root cron entry schedules the backup daily at 02:15, crond is running, and repeated end-to-end executions created dated archives containing the node2 validation file with a matching source/archive SHA-1. The backup also succeeded after both node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f9e74659480a4aed","cmd-2a990f9e045a4fc5","cmd-3840b490cfbf4f98","cmd-d3a542d8208f466f","cmd-41e38f007251418d"]},{"id":"R2","requirement":"Retain only the last seven days of dated inventory archives.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed script removes matching archives older than the seven-day window. A bounded boundary test directly showed an eight-day-old fixture removed and a six-day-old fixture retained; the retained fixture was then cleaned up.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f9e74659480a4aed","cmd-74801b113d734b06","cmd-aec8ef949ae14629"]},{"id":"R3","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Both nodes began on CentOS 5.11 with kernel 2.6.18-398.el5 and retained that kernel across their restarts and final lifecycle observations. The implementation used already-installed shell, SSH, tar, gzip, find, and cron facilities, with no package or OS upgrade commands present.","evidence_ids":["cmd-34d5d84cae8549a1","cmd-f46f065144174e08","cmd-3840b490cfbf4f98","cmd-d3a542d8208f466f","global:before_prepare:node1:platform","global:before_prepare:node2:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node2:platform"]}],"confidence":0.95,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.88,"summary":"The implementation mutations—hostname mappings, source/destination directories, restricted SSH credentials and forced command, backup scripts, and cron entry—have clear roles in the task. Retention-test archive fixtures were bounded and fully removed. Hygiene is reduced for three retained residues: cmd-f52c248d550f4632 created a validation fixture inside the source data and did not clean it, while cmd-76fcea2486e64208 and cmd-07d3a05a7ec44d56 created /tmp/inventory-reboot.log on node1 and node2 respectively; both logs remained in the final snapshots. No package changes, failed services, or new listening sockets were demonstrated.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f52c248d550f4632","cmd-76fcea2486e64208","cmd-07d3a05a7ec44d56","cmd-aec8ef949ae14629","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. The provider-wide restart protocol is also complete for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity corroborated by fixed lifecycle observations, recovered services and data, and a successful final end-to-end backup. Minor hygiene issues are limited to retained validation and reboot-log artifacts.","limitations":["No naturally triggered 02:15 cron run was observed; scheduling is supported by the installed cron entry and running daemon, while execution was verified through direct invocations.","The lifecycle package-health probe invoked an unsupported yum operation both before and after execution, so it cannot independently establish package consistency; the command timeline nevertheless contains no package mutation.","The post-executor platform snapshots directly confirm the unchanged kernel but do not print /etc/redhat-release; preservation of CentOS 5.11 is supported by the unchanged exact managed image identity and absence of OS-changing commands."]}
